          "question": "I have had a dry cough for about ten days after a cold, no fever. Should I be doing anything?",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nThe risk check flagged this as routine. It did not match any emergency pattern in the red-flag list. That is not a guarantee that nothing is wrong. It only means your description did not match the listed warning signs.\n\n**What the guidance says about a cold cough**\nThe common cold reference (Upper respiratory infection, General clinical reference) says that cough often lasts longer than the other cold symptoms, commonly two to three weeks. It describes this as normal. The antibiotic stewardship guidance (Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship and when antibiotics are indicated, guideline) adds that a cough lasting two to three weeks after a cold is expected. It also says that a cold lasting seven to ten days is running its course. Yellow or green mucus is not a sign of bacterial infection on its own.\n\n**What these sources did not cover**\nThe sources I searched do not tell you what to do for the cough itself, and they do not give treatment advice. They also do not say when a cough that has lasted longer than expected should be checked.\n\n**When to talk to a clinician**\nBased on the general guidance, it would be reasonable to speak with a doctor or pharmacist if the cough is getting worse instead of better, if you develop a fever or shortness of breath, or if it lasts well beyond three weeks. The influenza reference (Influenza, General clinical reference) notes that a sudden worsening after several days of improvement can be a sign of pneumonia, so a clear change for the worse is worth reporting.\n\n**Seek emergency care right away** if you have chest pain, trouble breathing, confusion, coughing up blood, or any symptoms that feel severe or sudden. Do not wait for a routine appointment in those cases.",
